टी. रेक्स के दंश का अंदाज़ा लगाने के लिए 2012 में कार्ल बेट्स और पीटर फ़ॉकिंगहैम ने एक बड़े वयस्क टी. रेक्स की खोपड़ी का 3D कंप्यूटर मॉडल बनाया। उन्होंने उसमें जबड़े की मांसपेशियाँ जोड़ीं। इसके लिए हड्डियों पर मांसपेशियों के निशानों और मगरमच्छों व चिड़ियों से तुलना का सहारा लिया। फिर मॉडल से "कटवाकर" देखा।

दूसरी टीमों ने अलग तरीके अपनाए और उन्हें थोड़े अलग जवाब मिले। पर सभी के हिसाब से टी. रेक्स किसी भी ज़िंदा जानवर से कहीं आगे है, और किसी भी और जाने-पहचाने ज़मीनी जानवर से भी।

{{story:dino-poop}}

In 1964, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson were testing a huge horn-shaped radio antenna at Bell Labs in New Jersey. They kept hearing a faint hiss that came from every part of the sky, day and night. They checked their equipment. They even scrubbed out pigeon droppings. The hiss stayed.

Other scientists had predicted that the universe's early glow should still be around, cooled and stretched into [[@radio-waves|radio waves]]. That is exactly what they had found. It is called the cosmic microwave background, and a small part of the fuzzy "snow" on old analogue televisions came from it.

{{story:radio-waves}}

हर सेकंड सूरज लगभग 60 करोड़ टन हाइड्रोजन को जोड़कर हीलियम बनाता है। इसमें लगभग 40 लाख टन पदार्थ ग़ायब हो जाता है, यानी शुद्ध ऊर्जा में बदल जाता है। अल्बर्ट [[@einstein|आइंस्टाइन]] का मशहूर [[@equations|समीकरण]], E = mc², समझाता है कि थोड़ा-सा द्रव्यमान ढेर सारी ऊर्जा कैसे बन जाता है।

यह ऊर्जा जल्दी बाहर नहीं निकल पाती। सूरज का अंदरूनी हिस्सा इतना ठसाठस भरा है कि रोशनी बार-बार सोखी और फिर छोड़ी जाती रहती है, और बेतरतीब दिशाओं में टकराती फिरती है। सतह तक पहुँचने में इसे हज़ारों-हज़ार साल लग सकते हैं। यानी तुम्हारे चेहरे को गर्माने वाली धूप ने अपना सफ़र पहले शहरों के बनने से भी बहुत पहले शुरू किया था।

{{story:photosynthesis}}

When an engine spins the big [[rotor|rotor]] one way, the helicopter's body wants to spin the other way. You can feel this on a swivel chair: twist your arms hard to the left and the chair turns right.

The small rotor on the tail pushes sideways to stop this. Pedals let the pilot change how hard it pushes, to turn the nose left or right. Some helicopters, like the big twin-rotor Chinook, have two main rotors turning in opposite directions instead, so their spins cancel out.

{{story:leonardo}}

The Indian pitta is a little smaller than a myna but painted in green, blue, black, white, yellow and bright red. Its Hindi name, navrang, means "nine colours". It spends winter in southern India and Sri Lanka, then moves north to forests in central India and the Himalayan foothills to breed when the rains come.

Pittas hop on the forest floor, flicking leaves aside to find insects and worms. In the monsoon, the damp leaf litter is full of them.

{{story:frogs-monsoon}}

लगभग 1 करोड़ साल में जानवरों की एक पूरी कतार समुद्र में और आगे बढ़ती गई। एम्बुलोसीटस, यानी "चलने वाली व्हेल", के बड़े पैर थे, और वह शायद [[@wild-otters-india|ऊदबिलाव]] की तरह तैरता था। बाद की किस्मों की टाँगें छोटी और पूँछ ज़्यादा ताक़तवर होती गई। लगभग 4 करोड़ साल पहले का बेसिलोसॉरस पूरी तरह समुद्र में रमा हुआ था, और उसकी पिछली टाँगें इतनी नन्ही थीं कि चलने के किसी काम की नहीं थीं।

उनकी नाक धीरे-धीरे सिर के ऊपर की ओर खिसकती गई और साँस लेने का छेद बन गई। उनकी रीढ़ मछली की तरह अगल-बगल नहीं, बल्कि दौड़ते कुत्ते की तरह ऊपर-नीचे मुड़ती रही।

{{story:india-drift}}

When chased, blackbucks often bound high into the air, sometimes about 2 metres up, before racing off. Scientists call this kind of jump *stotting* or pronking. One idea is that it tells a predator, "I'm strong. Don't bother." It may also warn the rest of the herd.

Adult males are striking. Their backs turn dark brown or nearly black, with a white belly and white rings around the eyes. Their horns twist upward in a corkscrew spiral, reaching up to about 75 cm, longer than a cricket bat's blade. Females are fawn-coloured and usually have no horns. Males clash horns over [[territory|territory]], but rarely hurt each other badly. A male's horns also hint at his age. Young males have short, straight spikes. The twists build up as he grows, and an old buck may have three to five full turns.

{{story:fastest-animal}}

A typical phone screen has about 2 to 3 million pixels. Each pixel has three sub-pixels: red, green and blue. Turn all three on fully and your eye sees white. Turn on red and green only and you see yellow. Our [[@eyes-see|eyes]] mix the tiny dots into a smooth picture because they are too small to see one by one.

There are two main screen types. In an LCD, a white backlight shines through liquid crystals that twist to let more or less light through coloured filters. In an OLED, each sub-pixel is a tiny light made of special carbon-based materials that glows by itself. OLED pixels can switch off completely, which gives deep blacks and saves battery.

{{story:eyes-see}}

In 1687, the English scientist [[@newton-life|Isaac Newton]] described a rule now called his first law of motion. A moving object keeps moving at the same speed, in the same direction, unless a force stops it.

You've felt this on a bus. When the driver brakes hard, you lurch forward. You weren't pushed. Your body just kept going while the bus slowed down. In a crash, the slowing happens in a split second, so the lurch is violent. At 50 km/h, an unbelted person hits whatever is in front of them about as hard as if they'd fallen from the top of a three-storey building.

{{story:newton-life}}

सबसे मशहूर बाउल थे लालन फ़कीर, जिन्हें लालन शाह भी कहते हैं। वे 1700 और 1800 के दशकों में आज के [[@bangladesh|बांग्लादेश]] के कुश्तिया के पास रहते थे। उनके शुरुआती जीवन के बारे में बहुत कम बातें पक्की हैं, और उन्होंने यह बताने से इनकार कर दिया कि वे किस धर्म में पैदा हुए थे। उनका एक गीत पूछता है कि जब हर कोई एक ही तरह पैदा होता और मरता है, तो लोग किसी की जाति या धर्म की इतनी परवाह क्यों करते हैं।

लालन ने सैकड़ों गीत रचे, जिन्हें उनके अनुयायियों ने ज़ुबानी याद कर लिया। कुश्तिया में उनकी दरगाह पर हर साल एक बड़ा मेला लगता है, जहाँ बाउल रात भर गाते हैं।

{{story:anthems}}

1970 के दशक में खख्खर को अपनी शैली मिल गई। उनके चित्र चटक और सपाट थे, मिठाई जैसे गुलाबी, हरे और पीले रंगों वाले, और अक्सर जानबूझकर सीधे-सादे दिखते थे। उनकी एक मशहूर कृति, *जनता वॉच रिपेयरिंग* (1972), में एक घड़ीसाज़ घड़ियों से घिरा अपनी छोटी-सी दुकान के काउंटर पर बैठा है। एक और, *मैन ईटिंग जलेबी* (1975), में ठीक वही है जो नाम कहता है।

उन्होंने कई जगहों से विचार लिए। भारतीय बाज़ारों में बिकने वाली देवी-देवताओं की सस्ती छपी तस्वीरों से उन्हें गाढ़े रंग और बाहरी रेखाएँ मिलीं। [[@andy-warhol|एंडी वॉरहॉल]] जैसे पश्चिमी पॉप कलाकारों ने दिखाया कि रोज़ की ज़िंदगी भी कला हो सकती है। शुरुआती इतालवी चित्रों ने, जिनमें किसी नज़ारे पर छोटी-छोटी आकृतियाँ बिखरी होती हैं, उन्हें एक ही तस्वीर में कई कहानियाँ कहना सिखाया।

{{story:andy-warhol}}

Around 508 BCE, the people of [[@democracy-athens|Athens]] began meeting on a hillside called the Pnyx to argue and vote on laws. Thousands could gather at once. But only free men born to citizen parents counted as citizens. Women, enslaved people and foreigners had no vote at all.

For most of history after that, kings and emperors ruled most of the world. Ideas of people's rule came back slowly, through [[parliament|parliaments]] in Britain, the [[@american-revolution|American Revolution]] and the [[@french-revolution|French Revolution]].

{{story:democracy-athens}}

You cannot ask every person in India what they spend, so experts ask a careful sample, just as a [[@census|census]] counts people and an opinion poll asks a few thousand voters. In India, the Household Consumption Expenditure Survey asks families what they spent on food, fuel, schooling and more.

From the answers, experts use [[statistics|statistics]] to work out the share of people below the line. They look at more than the [[@averages|average]], because a high average can hide many families who have very little.

{{story:surveys}}

The national emblem comes from a stone pillar top carved more than 2,200 years ago for the emperor [[@ashoka|Ashoka]] at Sarnath, near [[@varanasi|Varanasi]], where the Buddha gave his first sermon. Four lions sit back to back, facing the four directions. Below them, on a round base, are carvings of an elephant, a bull, a horse and a lion, separated by wheels.

In the flat version printed on coins and documents, only three lions show. On the base, a wheel sits in the middle with a bull on one side and a horse on the other. The words *Satyameva Jayate*, "Truth alone triumphs", are written below in Devanagari script. India adopted the emblem on 26 January 1950, the day [[@constitution|the Constitution]] began. The original carving is kept in the Sarnath Museum.

{{story:ashoka}}

Indian kitchens use [[@tomatoes|tomatoes]] almost every day. In July 2023, heavy rain and heat damaged crops in several growing regions. Fewer tomatoes reached markets, but families still wanted them. Prices in some cities shot past ₹200 a kilo, and some restaurants left tomatoes off their menus. A few months later, fresh harvests arrived and prices dropped back.

[[@onions-tears|Onions]] behave the same way. Because they matter so much to everyday cooking, sudden jumps in onion prices often make national news in India.

{{story:tomatoes}}

In 1936, an American magazine called the Literary Digest mailed out millions of ballots. More than 2 million came back, and they predicted that Alf Landon would beat President Franklin Roosevelt easily. Roosevelt won by a landslide.

The magazine had found names in telephone books and car registration lists. In the middle of the [[@great-depression|Great Depression]], people with phones and cars were richer than average, and the people who chose to reply were not typical either. Meanwhile a young pollster named George Gallup used a much smaller, carefully chosen sample and correctly called Roosevelt the winner. Polling changed for good.

{{story:elections-usa}}

सफ़ेद रक्त कोशिकाएँ शरीर की पहरेदार हैं। ये कई किस्म की होती हैं। कुछ बैक्टीरिया को साबुत निगल जाती हैं। कुछ [[antibody|एंटीबॉडी]] बनाती हैं, जो कीटाणुओं से चिपककर उन पर निशान लगा देती हैं ताकि उन्हें खत्म किया जा सके। बीमार पड़ने पर शरीर इन्हें और ज़्यादा बनाने लगता है।

प्लेटलेट्स इनसे भी छोटे होते हैं। जब खून की कोई नली फटती है, तो ये दौड़कर वहाँ पहुँचते हैं, आपस में चिपक जाते हैं और खून का थक्का बनाने में मदद करते हैं। फिर रेशों का एक जाल बनता है, जिसमें और कोशिकाएँ फँस जाती हैं। त्वचा पर यही सूखकर [[@scabs|पपड़ी]] बन जाता है।

{{story:heart-beat}}

1990 के दशक के आख़िर में लंदन के यूनिवर्सिटी कॉलेज में सारा-जेन ब्लेकमोर समेत एक टीम ने इसे परखने के लिए एक सीधा-सादा रोबोट बनाया। एक इंसान एक हाथ से लीवर हिलाता था, और [[@robots|रोबोट]] उसकी दूसरी हथेली पर फ़ोम का एक नरम टुकड़ा फिराता था।

जब रोबोट ठीक लीवर के साथ ही हिलता, तो मुश्किल से गुदगुदी होती। जब वैज्ञानिकों ने बस एक सेकंड के पाँचवें हिस्से की देरी जोड़ी, या उसकी दिशा बदल दी, तो गुदगुदी ज़्यादा लगी। दिमाग़ का अंदाज़ा जितना ज़्यादा चूका, छुअन उतनी ही किसी अजनबी की छुअन जैसी लगी।

{{story:robots}}

Greatbatch was working at the University of Buffalo on a gadget to record heart sounds. He needed a resistor, a small part that controls how much electricity flows. He reached into a box and pulled out one that was 100 times stronger than the one he wanted.

When he switched on, the circuit gave a short electrical squirt lasting less than two thousandths of a second. Then it rested for about one second and repeated. Greatbatch stared at it. He later said he knew instantly what it could do, because doctors had told him about patients whose hearts needed exactly that kind of steady nudge.

{{story:heart-beat}}

In 1932, The Times newspaper in London asked for a new typeface that was easy to read and saved space. The result was Times New Roman. In 1957, two Swiss designers made Helvetica, a plain, neat font that later appeared on signs, logos and subway maps around the world.

In 1994, Vincent Connare, a designer at Microsoft, saw a cartoon dog in a computer program talking in a stiff, serious font. He drew Comic Sans, based on the lettering in comic books, to look more relaxed. It became hugely popular. It also became one of the most mocked fonts ever, often used where it doesn't fit, such as on serious notices.

{{story:calligraphy}}

In 1773 Britain passed the Tea Act. It let the [[@east-india-company|East India Company]], which was struggling with debts, sell its tea in America cheaply and directly. The tea itself was grown in China at the time.

Many colonists saw this as a trick to make them accept Britain's right to tax them. On the night of 16 December 1773, a group of men in Boston, some loosely dressed as Mohawk Indians, boarded three ships and threw 342 chests of tea into the sea. It became known as the Boston Tea Party.

{{story:tea}}

[[@crocodiles-alligators|Alligators and crocodiles]] are cousins, but they rarely meet. Alligators like fresh water, and crocodiles prefer salty or brackish water near the coast. At the southern tip of Florida, fresh water from the Everglades mixes with sea water from Florida Bay. Both animals live there, making it the only place in the world where they share a home.

The [[@wetlands|wetlands]] are also a paradise for birds. Pink roseate spoonbills sweep their flat beaks through the water. Herons, egrets and storks stalk fish in the shallows. In winter, slow, gentle manatees gather in warmer coastal waters.

{{story:mangroves}}

The Surya Kund is about 50 metres long. Its sides are a maze of steps forming small triangles and diamonds, a bit like a [[@stepwells|stepwell]] turned into a giant puzzle. Little shrines are tucked among the steps, often counted as 108, to gods such as Ganesha, Shiva and Vishnu.

Worshippers once bathed in the tank before climbing up to the temple. In Gujarat's hot, dry summers, water was precious, and a tank like this also stored rain for the town. A great carved gateway called a torana once stood between the tank and the dance hall. Its tall pillars still frame the view for anyone climbing up from the water. From the bottom, the steps rise towards the dance hall like seats in a stadium.

{{story:konark}}

Visitors can walk down through the natural entrance, a steep trail dropping about 230 m, or ride an elevator. The elevator descends about 230 m (750 feet), as deep as a 75-storey building is tall.

The Big Room path is about 2 km long. Along it are formations with names like the Rock of Ages and the Bottomless Pit, which actually has a bottom about 40 m down. The stone icicles hanging from the ceiling are stalactites. The ones growing up from the floor are stalagmites. When the two meet, they form a column.

{{story:bats-fly}}

Pai and his team chose a subject, researched it in old texts and history books, and wrote a script panel by panel. Artists such as Ram Waeerkar, Pratap Mullick and Souren Roy then drew and inked the pages, and colourists finished them.

Each comic had to fit a lot of story into a small space. Pai insisted on research, sending writers to scholars and museums. He wanted clothes, weapons and buildings to look right for the period.

{{story:comics-art}}

Nagas are serpent beings who live in underwater palaces, guard treasures and control rain. Some are shown with human top halves and snake tails. People across India still honour snakes on Nag Panchami. The great serpent Shesha, with a thousand heads in some tellings, is the bed on which Vishnu rests.

Garuda, half man and half eagle, is the nagas' great rival. When the gods and demons churned the ocean, out came Airavata, a white elephant ridden by Indra, and Uchchaihshravas, a seven-headed flying horse.

{{story:myths-dragons}}

लोग कम से कम 5,000 साल से प्याज़ उगा रहे हैं। प्राचीन मिस्र के लोग क़ब्रों में प्याज़ रखते थे। फ़राओ रामसेस चतुर्थ, जिनकी मौत लगभग 3,100 साल पहले हुई थी, उनकी [[mummy|ममी]] की आँखों के गड्ढों में छोटे-छोटे प्याज़ रखे मिले।

{{story:mummies}}

भारत और चीन किसी भी और देश से कहीं ज़्यादा प्याज़ उगाते हैं, और 2024 में सबसे ज़्यादा प्याज़ भारत ने उगाया। महाराष्ट्र के नाशिक ज़िले का लासलगाँव एशिया की सबसे बड़ी प्याज़ मंडी है। भारत में प्याज़ इतना खाया जाता है कि जब ख़राब मौसम से फ़सल बिगड़ती है और [[@shops-markets|दाम]] उछलते हैं, तो यह अख़बारों के पहले पन्ने की ख़बर बन जाती है।
